The task is to analyze the performance of various indexing methods in a retrieval-as-you-go (RAS) setting with varying values of i and provide insights on their query time, recall rate, index size, and preprocessing time. The results are presented in Table II , where we show the average query time for each method at different levels of recall . 

The performance of all ANN indexes degrades significantly as igrows beyond O(1). In particular, for large values of i, these methods fail to retrieve the required point, resulting in near-zero recall. Moreover, their query time increases substantially, as the algorithms must iterate over an increasing number of candidates and compute distances for up to ipoints. 

In contrast, our algorithm, EpsHier , exhibits stable performance across all values of i, achieving fast query times and a guaranteed recall of 100% . Figure 18 compares the indexing (preprocessing) time and index size of EpsHier with those of the ANN indexes used in this experiment. We observe that both the index size and preprocessing time ofEpsHier are comparable to standard ANN methods. However, unlike ANN indexes, which do not meaningfully solve the RASproblem and exhibit zero recall for large i, our algorithm achieves a guaranteed recall of 100% . Figure 18 compares the indexing (preprocessing) time and index size of EpsHier with those of the ANN indexes used in this experiment. We observe that both the index size and preprocessing time ofEpsHier are comparable to standard ANN methods. However, unlike ANN indexes, which do not meaningfully solve the RASproblem and exhibit zero recall for large i, our algorithm achieves fast query times and a guaranteed recall of 
